Check GitHub repo structure

If GitHub opens directly to package.json, render.yaml, server/, client/, you are good.
If GitHub shows a folder named JobPulse/ first, then in Render use Blueprint path: JobPulse/render.yaml, or move the files out so render.yaml is at repo root. Render uses render.yaml at repo root by default, but lets you set a custom Blueprint path. (render.com)
Create/get Cloudinary URL

Cloudinary Console → Settings → API Keys
Copy the API environment variable, like:
cloudinary://API_KEY:API_SECRET@CLOUD_NAME
Cloudinary says this value includes cloud name, API key, and API secret, so keep it private. (cloudinary.com)

Deploy on Render

Render Dashboard → New → Blueprint
Connect your GitHub repo
Select branch
Deploy Blueprint
Fill Render secret variables
Render will ask for variables marked sync: false in render.yaml; that is expected for secrets. (render.com) Add:

MONGO_URI=your_mongodb_atlas_url
PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://your-render-url.onrender.com
PUBLIC_API_URL=https://your-render-url.onrender.com
CLOUDINARY_URL=cloudinary://API_KEY:API_SECRET@CLOUD_NAME
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=optional
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=optional
SMTP_PASS=your_gmail_app_password
After deploy
Open:

https://your-render-url.onrender.com/api/health
If it returns server running, log in to admin, upload an image, then check Cloudinary Media Library.

Important: if your real .env file was pushed to GitHub with passwords/API keys, remove it from GitHub and rotate those secrets. .env.example is fine to push; real .env should stay private.